#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "abb40124-5eda-57b4-a815-9cd8b5661da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5389" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the polar curve $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/8c25245d58ec9ddd8d8f1e73f8eae12c244ecd32018f939f1aba86b274622ba8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133481 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75c0168a-efdc-5162-a961-aae318783e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line Given an Acute Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "6272bafb-8e57-583f-a320-f066fe40184b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5390" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/245f03c8eeb5b84f7b4caafd61f37b7c15e77e77a447fc762170e05d61c87864.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the ray shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise angle that the ray makes with the positive $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\pi+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133482 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "04f489ae-768f-5e8e-bed6-0a88f481c54c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5391" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/1e1362c20f9e20bb22d049d8b218d786a5254603ab4cc36d90b5866095a8897c.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(6,-2\\sqrt{3})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2\\sqrt{3}}{6}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/id #uuid "e2c565be-0152-5088-8c4f-e66374cf2fb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5392" :question/problem "The polar curve $r=7$ and the ray $\\theta=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. What are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/728f7aad12a4f127cc002bc38c46fa87ba70d6ecd79ef311a0154014b649a0ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r, \\theta) = \\left(7, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(-\\dfrac 72, -\\dfrac{7\\sqrt 3}2\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b49f5b-5f7f-5561-a4ba-04923fe783c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/479df1b8aafdc4c061d221cbf5079958289178d982a33bb118934850ff889373.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280781} {:answer/id #uuid "abeca79b-2d2f-5208-b4a0-d768650ecb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/82bf2fb643852163329b767d2cf8767c9ed8411060092f1652143e3842727ca2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280782} {:answer/id #uuid "642e1d01-23fe-5517-8c66-4d309936f848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/423d9cb637371b49b351145113d40905ea786029c244ac949ee5059ea5c7315b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280783} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8bbd44-f046-5240-ba16-470cf5e6bc87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280784} {:answer/id #uuid "9982890b-bf04-586c-8f35-25f4d659b74b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/7931fc