a8046e0-eb6f-5bef-bcda-247cd7ca6dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280847} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aaed982-f05e-512a-b61d-1f879d3dc05a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0b99479-a2a8-50dc-ae3c-121887072818" :question/math-academy-id "q-321889" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/e64215da53594d7e7bc24f8df953aa84f0648cd3ab58d0a8b8005be653c28902.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$ \n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{(-3)}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the second quadrant. So, we find the angle in the second quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e75f4e7-c624-5a37-8573-6423751d0580" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280850} {:answer/id #uuid "b515d14b-3dc4-5631-b0ea-378bb15eda9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b515d14b-3dc4-5631-b0ea-378bb15eda9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280851} :answer-field/id #uuid "5124b588-8a3c-5ccf-8866-a4931718f984" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280849} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8b61b7-537f-5908-8cbd-9d7d655f5c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c8b61b7-537f-5908-8cbd-9d7d655f5c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280853} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6a5ab0-404c-5ae7-9c71-fb24a8775706" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d010d4ee-1d75-5b6c-b917-04a660e049da" :question/math-academy-id "q-321918" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/9af86b077257cd7a6052de0a0a89d720935eace0ac87dd0bf1afc581ce84c60d.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=5$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ intersect at the point $Q,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $Q$ are $(r,\\theta )=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $Q$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc8d4944-9005-5712-ad88-744dac83603e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Point of Intersection of a Circle and a Radial Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d5b3af-afa4-5db2-9471-7809994c7946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d5b3af-afa4-5db2-9471-7809994c7946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280856} :answer-field/id #uuid "2726ac69-dc5e-55b0-8fd5-b45373a8ddfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280855} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c60cf1-f82c-5f52-9a5a-01cb41bbe52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c60cf1-f82c-5f52-9a5a-01cb41bbe52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280858} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd8fce3-5111-54f6-8829-36c2c9106cb0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe0dfb5a-6d65-5994-a697-3c4fd1cac5cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-321970" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/5e29d99fc6ae3a848bfe59ab9e77595ab9ca2a605d0d03139e8599adf15e6fd9.png)\n\nThe polar curve $r=9$ and the ray $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ intersect at the point $P,$ as shown above. The Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "The polar coordinates of $P$ are $(r,\\theta )=(9,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We can convert the polar coordinates of $P$ to Cartesian coordinates, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efdcf273-d029-50dd-b0ab-22d608136ff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/10d1cdb8ecbfe7f7153618b5da57fecefbf8711d395cf3c6f5c879f3169977f9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280861} {:answer/id #uuid "ed89b2d4-a7d4-5ae2-b904-95e808b44b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/85271401b413b162990a99e905ebecbfb4048953e77bcb5808c0b729c1fc7de3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280862} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdf7f88-5784-5659-8bd1-9436ad73c9ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980294/assets/aa72b03a85b16e3a9ef9dc3f65f4126cdee0339ac722fcfe8a1f21973796f62